{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"009d7b4422e1bbba","name":"card_event_detailed_recon","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Germany/OceaniaStorm/events/card_event_detailed_recon","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"DETAILED RECON","text":"Cost is reduced by the amount spent operating this turn. Draw a card, reduce its cost by the same amount.","flavorText":"The Allied Convoy PQ 17 sailed from Iceland in 1942 with 35 merchant ships carrying tanks, aircraft, vehicles, ammunition, and other supplies for the Soviet Union. German forces found the convoy on 1 July, then shadowed it with submarines and aircraft. The Luftwaffe had Ju 88 bombers, He 111 torpedo bombers, He 115 floatplanes, Fw 200 Condors, BV 138 flying boats, and other reconnaissance aircraft ready for Arctic operations, based in northern Norway. The German plan, called Operation Rösselsprung, involved the battleship Tirpitz, heavy cruisers Admiral Scheer, Lützow and Admiral Hipper, and destroyers. Their possible sortie caused deep concern in the British Admiralty. On 4 July, believing a surface attack was imminent, Admiral Dudley Pound ordered the Allied cruiser force to withdraw and the convoy to scatter. The German battleships never reached the convoy, but the scattered merchant ships lost their protection. Luftwaffe aircraft and U-boats then attacked the isolated ships across the Barents Sea. Only 11 merchant ships reached Soviet ports. The losses included 24 ships, 430 tanks, 210 aircraft, and 3,350 vehicles.","faction":"Germany","type":"order","rarity":"Common","cost":4,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_event_detailed_recon","title":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_event_detailed_recon_title","SourceString":"DETAILED RECON","LocalizedString":"DETAILED RECON"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::Germany","kredits":4,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_event_detailed_recon_text","SourceString":"Cost is reduced by the amount spent operating this turn. Draw a card, reduce its cost by the same amount.","LocalizedString":"Cost is reduced by the amount spent operating this turn. Draw a card, reduce its cost by the same amount."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_event_detailed_recon_history","SourceString":"The Allied Convoy PQ 17 sailed from Iceland in 1942 with 35 merchant ships carrying tanks, aircraft, vehicles, ammunition, and other supplies for the Soviet Union. German forces found the convoy on 1 July, then shadowed it with submarines and aircraft. The Luftwaffe had Ju 88 bombers, He 111 torpedo bombers, He 115 floatplanes, Fw 200 Condors, BV 138 flying boats, and other reconnaissance aircraft ready for Arctic operations, based in northern Norway. The German plan, called Operation Rösselsprung, involved the battleship Tirpitz, heavy cruisers Admiral Scheer, Lützow and Admiral Hipper, and destroyers. Their possible sortie caused deep concern in the British Admiralty. On 4 July, believing a surface attack was imminent, Admiral Dudley Pound ordered the Allied cruiser force to withdraw and the convoy to scatter. The German battleships never reached the convoy, but the scattered merchant ships lost their protection. Luftwaffe aircraft and U-boats then attacked the isolated ships across the Barents Sea. Only 11 merchant ships reached Soviet ports. The losses included 24 ships, 430 tanks, 210 aircraft, and 3,350 vehicles.","LocalizedString":"The Allied Convoy PQ 17 sailed from Iceland in 1942 with 35 merchant ships carrying tanks, aircraft, vehicles, ammunition, and other supplies for the Soviet Union. German forces found the convoy on 1 July, then shadowed it with submarines and aircraft. The Luftwaffe had Ju 88 bombers, He 111 torpedo bombers, He 115 floatplanes, Fw 200 Condors, BV 138 flying boats, and other reconnaissance aircraft ready for Arctic operations, based in northern Norway. The German plan, called Operation Rösselsprung, involved the battleship Tirpitz, heavy cruisers Admiral Scheer, Lützow and Admiral Hipper, and destroyers. Their possible sortie caused deep concern in the British Admiralty. On 4 July, believing a surface attack was imminent, Admiral Dudley Pound ordered the Allied cruiser force to withdraw and the convoy to scatter. The German battleships never reached the convoy, but the scattered merchant ships lost their protection. Luftwaffe aircraft and U-boats then attacked the isolated ships across the Barents Sea. Only 11 merchant ships reached Soviet ports. The losses included 24 ships, 430 tanks, 210 aircraft, and 3,350 vehicles."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Common","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::OceaniaStorm","usedTriggers":["ERegisteredCardFunction::OnEndOfTurn","ERegisteredCardFunction::OnOtherCardOperationKreditSpent"],"soundEnterPlay":{"ObjectName":"SoundWave'War_UI_Drum_Movement_Field_Snares_3'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/War_UI/Waves/Drum_Movements/War_UI_Drum_Movement_Field_Snares_3.0"},"historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/DETAILED_RECON.DETAILED_RECON","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_event_detailed_recon_photo","SourceString":"A German U-boat preparing to leave Kiel, Germany, during Operation Rösselsprung in 1942.","LocalizedString":"A German U-boat preparing to leave Kiel, Germany, during Operation Rösselsprung in 1942."},"OnAttackVisualsSoft":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Blueprints/Effects/BP_AttackOrder.BP_AttackOrder_C","SubPathString":""}},"variables":[{"name":"costPaid","type":"int32","direction":"in"}],"functions":[{"name":"ExecuteUbergraph_card_event_detailed_recon","owner":"card_event_detailed_recon_C","params":[{"name":"EntryPoint","type":"int32","direction":"in"}],"pure":false,"event":false,"flags":"FUNC_Final | FUNC_UbergraphFunction | FUNC_HasDefaults","locals":[{"name":"Temp_int_Loop_Counter_Variable","type":"int32","direction":"in"},{"name":"CallFunc_Add_IntInt_ReturnValue","typ